From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:54:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(llama-cpp): a BERT text pair for embedding and rerank
 (pairs:true), and rerank_score:sigmoid

Carried as a llama.cpp patch beside the others
(patches/0026-bert-text-pairs.patch), after 0025.

MedCPT's article encoder reads a (title, abstract) pair and its
cross-encoder a (query, article) pair, each as Hugging Face's pair
tokenizer builds it: `[CLS] a [SEP] b [SEP]`, the second segment on
token-type row 1, the two cut longest first to max_length. llama.cpp gave
every token row 0, its rerank prompt for a WordPiece vocabulary had no
closing [SEP], and its converter dropped a BERT classifier's pooler.

With the model option `pairs:true` (VeGa's wrapper already sends pairs only
to a model declaring it):
- Embedding: VeGa's pair marker followed by the JSON [first, second] is
  built into `[CLS] a [SEP] b [SEP]` (text_pair.h), cut longest first to the
  bound (the load's context, the physical batch, the trained positions:
  512 for MedCPT), as Hugging Face's TruncationStrategy::LongestFirst - 216
  of 216 rows of its own table agree; the context reads the segments
  (pair_segments, the inner patch). A first segment holding [SEP] is
  refused, so is one text with a [SEP] inside it: the graph would start a
  second segment there.
- Rerank: (query, document) is built the same way.
- `rerank_score:sigmoid` answers the logit's sigmoid, as TEI's /rerank and
  the OpenVINO backend gave MedCPT's; `raw`, the default, the logit.
A llama.cpp without pair_segments (a fork) refuses a load asking pairs:true:
a pair read as one segment is a wrong vector. Without the option nothing
changes.

Measured on CPU (this tree, both models converted with it, f32) through the
gRPC server, against NCBI's usage (transformers, fp32, CPU): the article
encoder, 67 PubMed pairs (three past 512 tokens, one per truncation case):
cosine 0.9999882 at worst, the token counts Hugging Face's; without the
segments 0.9736. The cross-encoder, 80 (title, abstract) pairs: sigmoid
scores within 0.0075, logits within 0.079, the order of every pair the same
but for one tie (two records with the same abstract); without pairs, logits
off by up to 18.9. Not run on a card.

